00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:58.010
- Yes.
- Because I studied some NLP

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:02.240
and question-answer systems
are very difficult to do properly.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.950
Can you tell us a little bit
about the AI that was involved.

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:07.440
Why don't you tell us
what happened, Manish.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.190
What is some of the AI that was involved?

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:18.170
- Yeah, so let me show you the next set of demo
and I'll highlight what we are building in that.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:22.950
- Okay.
So, if you go up there, Malesh, to the questions.

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:23.720
- Yeah.
- This is good.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:24.380
- Yeah.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:27.630
- So, the question
is a rattle snake venomous?

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:34.810
And, you know, of course, we are able
to extract the answers, but look at the highlighting.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:37.410
So, what we are doing is
really reading comprehension.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:44.290
We are first, extracting the passages
or pieces of information

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:46.440
from the documents
that contain the answer.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:47.950
But that's just
the first step.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:54.940
The second step of reading comprehension
is we are then extracting the specific answer

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:57.500
from that passage
and highlighting it right here

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:59.060
so we can say the answer

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:01.280
to the question
is rattle snake venomous?

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.490
We are highlighting the specific answer.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:11.390
If you go down, you'll see the next question
being, "Is King Cobra venomous?"

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:14.210
And here, we are actually
building an inference model.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:19.990
Which is not just extracting the answer,
but really able to infer the answer,

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:22.840
and again, going back to the point
of saving users time.

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:27.920
So, here, you'll see yes at the top,
and that's coming from our inference model,

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:29.860
and we are now saying, that yes,

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:33.200
based off of the information
we found in the document,

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.280
the model is able
to really infer that knowledge

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:40.150
and answer the question
directly right there.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:45.250
- Which is more next level, because it's,
I mean, it's pretty good feat

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:46.950
to be able to look across the documents

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.040
that are accessible to Bing
to answer a question,

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:50.750
and, then, highlight where the answer is.

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:54.280
But then, to go further and say, yeah,
and here's our evidence

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:56.290
is pretty amazing.
- Yeah.

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:58.880
And going back to that point,
because it's a hard problem.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:02.750
Look at the third example,
"Is garter snake venomous?"

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:07.180
And in this example, you know,
although, we have the answer,

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.400
the answer is not very straight forward.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:13.130
It's not a yes-no-type of answer
because the passage is saying

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:16.220
that although common garter snakes
are not considered venomous,

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:19.250
they have a gland
which produces some venom, right?

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:22.000
- So, it's not very obvious.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:26.680
And our model can now really understand
where, you know,

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:30.070
how they infer knowledge
from the pieces of text

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:32.000
for a query to answer the question

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.950
when to answer the question
directly or show the passage,

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:40.670
and you know, there are
several parts of AI

00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:43.250
that has gone into building our system.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:48.130
If you think about conversational
Q&amp;A, there are four big pieces.

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:51.240
One, how do you understand
the conversation, right?

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:55.090
Which is the case of Singapore
history and ethnicity.

00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:58.500
We need to determine whether
that this question requires context

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:00.050
from previous questions or not.

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:03.210
And the second is, if it does,
how do you take the context

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:05.710
and understand the question, right?

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:10.220
- So, what does that look like, for example,
if I'm going to ask a random question

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:11.440
into the bot, you know,

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.170
"Who invented the printing press?"

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:15.750
"How old was he when he died?"

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:17.710
- Yeah.
- Is there some kind of stack

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:19.280
that's being generated in order

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:22.200
to remember
who the "he" is, right?

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:23.410
- Yeah.
- How do you do that?

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:24.300
- That's a great question.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:28.260
So, there are three parts of that system.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:29.050
- Okay.

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:31.740
- The first part is we store context.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:36.830
Which is, we call context as previous questions
and answers that we gave the user.

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:43.000
So, the questions that users asked and the answers
we provided in that session, so, that's our first part.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:48.050
Second part is our lead
model which is an R&amp;N,

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:50.520
it can take the context
and the current question

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:52.600
and reformulate the current question

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:54.250
to make it fully independent.

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:59.520
And the third part is
once you have re-formulated into,

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:01.580
you know, multiple
possible options,

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:03.140
you know, replacing he with,

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:05.450
let's say the person,

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:08.270
exactly, the person
that's being talked about.

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:10.460
How do you know that you
re-formulated correctly

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:13.010
because there could be
several different options, right?

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:15.430
So, that's our third part,
that's sort of meat and potatoes

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:17.950
for what we do in Relevance.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:23.060
And we are now, we look at
the post-web signals to really figure out

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:26.980
how do you pick the re-formulation
that makes the most sense.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:32.440
- So, it looks like the three-step process
is number one, you need to get the question out.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:34.610
Then, first you need to know
what the question is, then,

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:36.510
if there's any indirect
object pronouns,

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.350
for example, you need to replace them.

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:39.780
You need to replace them
in an intelligent way.

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:41.880
I'm assuming you'll have multiple candidates.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:42.960
- Exactly.
- That happens, and then,

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:44.800
once you use your inference
engine to find out,

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.020
then you go and answer
the question like you did.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:48.670
- Right.
I mean the first step there,

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:50.690
you have to extract
the entities and concepts.

00:10:50.690 --> 00:10:52.130
- Sure.
- From the questions, right?

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:56.040
And so, that's also where a lot of work
that has gone into the Bing stack.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:58.760
- Yeah.
- So, what does that look like?

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:03.950
First of all, Bing has got to be
this tremendous area of information

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.800
where you can get all this data out of,

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:08.300
but you have also the riches of data

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:09.860
that you have to search through,

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:13.140
but it's unclear to me
like how you're using Bing

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:14.360
to answer the questions

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:17.090
and then what are you doing
with the questions themselves,

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:19.630
because you have to, there's a lot of
understanding the questions, right?

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:20.800
Why don't you tell us about that.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:21.600
- Absolutely, absolutely.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:28.080
So, there are several parts of this system
where we are truly leveraging Bing.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:34.080
But we're also building some really amazing piece
of AI technology to solve the problems.

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:35.860
So, let me explain.

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:39.400
The first piece is
the conversational understanding.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:42.970
Given a context, how do you
understand the question?

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:45.250
As simple as that.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:48.460
It sounds pretty simple but there are
a lot of models that are being used

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:50.120
and we use Bing Stack

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:52.760
to the fullest in this area.

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:57.320
For example, if the question,
if the question you said,

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:01.260
"Who acted in Star Wars
Episode Five?"

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:04.020
And the next question is,
"Who directed it?"

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:07.860
Or, "When was he born?" Right?

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:09.800
So, there are a lot of pronouns

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:11.740
in these second questions

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:14.490
and these are very easier, easy cases

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:16.680
because when there is pronoun,

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.260
you can make the argument

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:22.000
that pronoun needs to be
resolved through the context

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:23.360
that was there earlier.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:27.800
So, we have, you know,
code reference resolution models, we have deep models,

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:29.780
trained using a lot training data

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:32.840
that can take the context
from previous questions

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:35.380
and resolve the current question

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:36.680
to understand the question better.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:39.040
But that's just
the first part.

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:40.690
You know, once you understand
the question better,

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:44.660
the next big part is how do you
retrieve the answer?

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:47.180
- Right.
- So, for that,

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:48.840
as well, we fully leverage Bing.

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:52.540
So, we get the results, Bing today
is already very good

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:56.510
at saying what documents are good
documents for a question, right?

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:02.270
But, what we do on top of it,
is we break down the document barriers,

00:13:02.270 --> 00:13:04.950
we break it down
into pieces of information

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:06.830
that those documents contain,

00:13:06.830 --> 00:13:10.520
and then, rank those
pieces of information.

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:15.230
Let's make it simple, call it a passage,
so a document can have different passages.

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:17.900
We rank the passages,
we pick the best passage.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:20.340
So, that's where we use Bing a lot.

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:23.420
But then, the very next step
is once you have the passage,

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:25.890
how do you extract
the specific answer.

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:30.130
And that's really reading comprehension
and we've built retention models that can,

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:32.230
you know, train on a lot,

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:35.050
a lot of training data
to given a question

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:38.200
and a passage,
extract the appropriate answer.

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:44.970
And the last step, which is also very important
is once you have extracted the answer for a question,

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:46.190
how do you machine writing?

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:49.420
How do you say it in a
very natural language way

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:52.700
so that the users can trust the answer
that you've provided.

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:57.050
For example, if the question is, "What is the net
worth of Bill Gates."

00:13:57.050 --> 00:13:59.860
And if a machine
just blurts out an answer

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:01.610
which is $300 billion.

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:05.490
You know, you might be
wondering was that really...

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:07.870
- Was that really Bill Gates,
or was that the other question?

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:08.580
- Exactly.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:12.800
And then, what we are doing, is we're building
a model that can machine write

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:14.350
which can take the question

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:15.860
and the answer
and can convert it

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:17.690
into a very natural
language response.

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:25.530
It can say something like, "According to this website,
I found that Bill Gates net worth is $320 billion."

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:28.700
And that is a much natural response

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:30.840
that users can understand,

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:34.290
they know the source of where
the information came from,

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:38.480
and they know that they know
something that they can trust.

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:40.900
- And that's, I mean,
that's pretty amazing right?

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:42.370
Because, once you've done essentially,

00:14:42.370 --> 00:14:46.120
you've taken what Bing's really good
at returning documents,

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:47.980
you've actually narrowed it down

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:49.750
to Bing returning Passages

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:51.690
and then they're using
your machine learning models

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:54.840
to infer the proper answer
with the correct context.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:56.100
Is that about the sum of it?

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:56.670
- Yes.

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:58.580
And you do this at a web scale, right?

00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:03.770
That is where the Bing's strength in dealing
with the entire Internet comes into play.

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:06.360
And this is a very general
technology that we are building.

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:10.330
So, you know, it's really not specific
to only one type of document.

00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:14.750
It's a general models that we are building
that can be leveraged across the Internet.

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:17.680
- So, is this something that's available now.

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:21.970
For example, let's just say I want to open
a home improvement store,

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:24.870
and I want my bot
to be able to answer stuff

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:27.340
based upon stuff on my own website.

00:15:27.340 --> 00:15:29.120
Is that something that can be done?

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.210
- It could be absolutely done.

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:34.140
It's just that we have to enable it for that website.

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:36.570
- Sure.
- We have the information about that website

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:39.500
if it is a public domain website,
we have that information.

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:43.680
It could be very easily turned on
from our site to answer this question

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.620
based on that website.

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:46.470
So, that's the beauty of it.

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:51.190
We can modify the entire web without it,
the web-masters having to do anything.

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:55.390
That information is already there,
we can do machine reading comprehension and machine

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:56.590
writing on top of it

00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:58.030
and give you the answers
that you need.

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:01.870
So at any time, I can have any one
chat up my website, it'd be like,

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:04.410
"Hey, so tell me about
your terms of service,

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.390
how long do I have to block?"

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:09.020
Then, from this particular
type of technology,

00:16:09.020 --> 00:16:10.740
the bot will be able answer

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:12.850
succinctly and give you the passage as well.

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:15.500
- That's correct.
- That's pretty amazing.

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:18.340
So, when will this type of stuff be available?

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:21.730
- So, we are heavily experimenting right now.

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:22.560
- Okay.

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:27.720
- You know, in some sense,
we're really truly creating a new category of product,

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:30.560
a new direction of where
we want the search to go.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:34.460
We want search to become
truly intelligent, truly useful.

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:38.450
We want to save users time
and that takes a lot of iterations.

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:44.520
We are, as I said, experimenting a lot,
tweaking the experience,

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:46.810
understanding how users are reacting,

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:48.360
and what's working
and what's not.

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:55.710
But, and, you know, as soon as we get to a point
where we feel pretty good about how users are behaving,

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:57.710
and what information
we are providing,

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:00.090
and whether they are liking it
or not, we'll release it.

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:01.610
It might be sooner than you think.

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:02.540
- That's amazing.

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:06.950
I'm excited for one day I'm going to have my own shop
and I'm just going to ask my computer,

00:17:06.950 --> 00:17:10.320
"Tell me, how do I make my own robot?"

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:12.110
And then, your robot is going to tell me

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:14.880
how to make a robot
and we will have robot inception.

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:16.240
[Laughter] Thanks so much,

00:17:16.850 --> 00:17:18.830
Manish and Nilesh for spending
some time with us on,

00:17:18.830 --> 00:17:21.150
"How to make search more intuitive."

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:23.340
It's been awesome, thanks for watching,
and we'll see you next time.

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:24.590
- Thank you.
- Thanks.

